  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nA series of relatively weak short wave troughs will move through\nthe mean westerly flow aloft through Wednesday. The result will\nbe scattered showers (with some snowflakes in the higher\nterrain) from time to time but no washouts. However, with the \ncolder air aloft the skies will average mostly cloudy, \nespecially in the mountains. NW winds will be a bit breezy today\nwith gusts 20 to 25 MPH at times. Daytime temperatures over the\nnext few days will actually average out a little above normal.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nA deep trof axis will move across the Great Lakes on Thursday as a \nsfc low develops near the OH or TN Valley and then swings offshore \nof the U.S. East Coast Thursday night into Friday. This system is \nthen progged to move northeastward Friday night into Saturday while \ndeepening and becoming closed off. Forecast uncertainty in the \nultimate track of this area of low pressure remains high, with some \nmodel runs bringing it inland over New England while other guidance \nkeeps the low well to the SE of Cape Cod. The last several runs of \nthe deterministic GFS have been a bit of an outlier with showing a \nmore inland track, as low center clustering from the EPS, GEFS, and \nmost AI guidance keeps the low offshore... and in some cases so \noffshore we that impacts would be little to none. \n\nGiven this uncertainty, don't see any reason to deviate from the NBM \nas it shows a reasonable middle ground approach. As is often the \ncase, should snow occur the most likely locations would be across \nthe mtns and towards the Canadian Border. Gusty winds and rain are \npossible elsewhere. Of higher confidence is for cooler temperatures \nto arrive late week into the upcoming weekend as the deep trof \nsettles over New England.",
